.TE
.PP
Persist the combined startup sequence across future sessions by setting
\f[CR]init_moods\f[R] inside an active Koshka session.
.IP
.EX
koshconf set init_moods kosh,bash \-\-persist
.EE
.PP
Inspect \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] before persisting \f[CR]init_moods\f[R] if
the configuration was generated with \f[CR]koshconf create kosh\f[R].
That preset already sources Bash startup files directly inside
\f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R].
Enable only one loading mechanism so the Bash startup files are not
executed twice.
.SH EXECUTE BASH SCRIPTS THAT FAIL STRICT VALIDATION
Run a Bash script under the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood when the default
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood rejects its syntax.
.IP
.EX
kosh \-\-mood bash script.bash
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-mood bash\f[R] flag selects the Bash compatibility mood
for that execution.
If the \f[CR]bash\f[R] binary remains installed on the host,
\f[CR]bash script.bash\f[R] and a script started as a command with a
\f[CR]#!/bin/bash\f[R] shebang run under the installed Bash, unless
mimicry (\f[CR]\-I\f[R]) runs the script inside Koshka.
.SH SET KOSHKA AS THE DEFAULT LOGIN SHELL
.IP "1." 3
Verify that the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] binary is reachable in \f[CR]PATH\f[R]
and executes cleanly from the current Bash session.
.IP
.EX
command \-v kosh
kosh \-\-version
.EE
.IP "2." 3
Register the absolute path of \f[CR]kosh\f[R] in \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R]
and update the user login shell with \f[CR]chsh\f[R] on Unix systems.
.IP
.EX
kosh_path=\[dq]$(command \-v kosh)\[dq]
grep \-Fx \[dq]$kosh_path\[dq] /etc/shells \f[B]||\f[R] printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[dq]$kosh_path\[dq] \f[B]|\f[R] sudo tee \-a /etc/shells
chsh \-s \[dq]$(command \-v kosh)\[dq]
.EE
.IP "3." 3
Sign out of the system session and sign back in to activate the new
login shell.
If a terminal emulator overrides the system login shell with a custom
command, update the profile settings inside that terminal application.
On Windows, \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R] and \f[CR]chsh\f[R] are not used.
Configure Koshka directly as a terminal profile on Windows.
.SH REVERT THE LOGIN SHELL TO BASH
.IP "1." 3
Keep the \f[CR]bash\f[R] binary installed on the system until migration
testing is complete.
.IP "2." 3
Confirm from an active Bash session that the absolute path of
\f[CR]bash\f[R] is present in \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R], then assign
\f[CR]bash\f[R] as the login shell.
.IP
.EX
chsh \-s \[dq]$(command \-v bash)\[dq]
.EE
.IP "3." 3
Sign out and sign back in to restore Bash as the active login shell.
.IP "4." 3
Remove any direct Koshka startup commands from terminal emulator
profiles before deleting the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] binary.
.SH UNINSTALL THE KOSHKA BINARY
.IP "1." 3
Revert the system login shell and terminal emulator profiles to another
shell before removing Koshka.
.IP "2." 3
Remove the binary using the installation method that matches the host
environment.
.PP
.TS
